  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest radar and satellite imagery reveal a large area of \ncloud cover and occasional light rain showers and/or drizzle \nextending from the Midwest to the Mid-Atlantic Coast, associated\nwith an elongated surface trough to the north of a stationary \nfront. Initially, periodic light rain and/or drizzle is expected\ntoday, generally east of the I-71 corridor further away from \nthe better forcing. However, this surface trough will gradually \nmove west across the Ohio Valley into Wednesday as high pressure\nbegins building south across the Eastern Great Lakes, resulting\nin more widespread showers overnight into Wednesday afternoon. \nConfidence is increasing for a few thunderstorms to accompany \nthis activity, especially near the I-71 corridor as the latest \nguidance indicates a narrow swath of elevated instability > 500 \nJ/kg. The weak forcing in place should limit rainfall amounts to\nless than an inch, though localized higher amounts, especially \nin thunderstorms, remain possible. Rain chances will quickly \ndiminish Wednesday evening and overnight as high pressure \ncontinues to build south into the area. \n\nPeriodic rain showers and cloud cover will contribute to below \naverage daytime temperatures in the 60s or perhaps even upper 50s at \ntimes and lows in the 50s or upper 40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure will build south across the Great Lakes by the \nend of the week and persist through the weekend. Initially, high\npressure and the departing chilly air mass could result in a \nfavorable scenario for some lows in the upper 30s to develop, \nparticularly across inland Northwest Pennsylvania, though 40s \nshould be more common elsewhere. The air mass will gradually \nmoderate through the weekend with high pressure in place. \nTemperatures will return to the upper 60s to low 70s by Sunday \nand perhaps even higher into the mid to upper 70s by early next \nweek.",
